	year = {2026},
	journal = {Results in Engineering},
	volume = {32},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1016/j.rineng.2026.112910},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050413526?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {School of Advanced Technologies, Iran University of Science and Technology, Tehran, Iran},
	abstract = {The accelerating deployment of solar power plants (SPPs) worldwide has introduced an expanding attack surface at the intersection of cyber infrastructure and physical energy systems. Existing risk assessment frameworks for SPPs have predominantly focused on either physical reliability or cybersecurity in isolation, failing to capture the cascading dynamics that characterize modern cyber–physical threats. This paper presents the Resilience-based Cyber–Physical Risk (R-CPR) quantification model, a Bayesian Network (BN) framework that is primarily empirically parameterized using maximum likelihood estimation from historical incident data, complemented by domain expertise where data are insufficient. The model captures the interplay among exogenous conditions, threat events, proactive controls, reactive responses, and dual impact dimensions—financial cost and recovery time—within a five-level hierarchical directed acyclic graph (DAG) structure. The DAG structure is validated through data-driven structure learning using the PC algorithm and conditional independence testing, confirming that the expert-defined causal assumptions are consistent with empirical evidence. The model is parameterized using 147 real-world SPP cyber–physical incidents collected from multiple international sources, spanning five geographic regions and four plant-scale categories. Laplace smoothing (pseudo-count α = 1) is applied to ensure conditional probability table (CPT) robustness in the presence of data sparsity. Formal statistical analysis confirms the weak correlation between financial cost and recovery time (Pearson r = 0.187, p = 0.024), validating the dual impact node structure. Benchmark comparisons demonstrate that the R-CPR BN outperforms a conventional BN, logistic regression, and random forest classifiers across Brier Score (0.142 vs. 0.198–0.217), AUC-ROC (0.891 vs. 0.784–0.832), and accuracy (0.837 vs. 0.748–0.792) metrics. Prescriptive analysis reveals that strengthening proactive controls from low to high maturity reduces the probability of high-impact outcomes by approximately 33%, while reactive response improvements alone yield a complementary 19% reduction. These findings demonstrate that the R-CPR model provides a robust, data-driven, and actionable framework for cyber–physical risk management in solar energy infrastructure. © 2026 The Author(s).},
