	affiliations = {Politecnico di Milano, Department of Energy, Milan, 20156, Italy},
	abstract = {Accurate very short-term Direct Normal Irradiance (DNI) nowcasting is essential for the operational efficiency of Concentrating Solar Power (CSP) plants, where rapid sun-cloud occlusions can lead to significant thermal stress and energy losses. This study proposes a multi-modal nowcasting framework that combines thermal All-Sky Infrared (ASI) image sequences with synchronous meteorological data to predict DNI at 10- and 15-minute horizons. Four advanced deep learning architectures are systematically benchmarked: baseline Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and Residual Network (ResNet) for deep spatial encoding, CNN-Long Short-Term Memory (CNN-LSTM) for spatiotemporal dynamics, and CNN-Transformer for long-range attention-based dependencies. Evaluated on a rigorously selected 33-day test set, the hybrid CNN-LSTM architecture emerged as the most robust model, achieving a coefficient of determination (R2) of 0.7998 and 0.7688 for the 10- and 15-minute horizons, respectively, and reaching peak daily forecast skill (FS) values exceeding 50%. Crucially, targeted evaluation of high-volatility transients demonstrated the model's superior ability to proactively anticipate severe irradiance ramps (> 100 W/m2/min). The statistical robustness of these predictive gains over the persistence baseline was strictly validated via the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (p < 10-140). Furthermore, modality comparisons confirmed that thermal infrared imaging effectively mitigates solar glare, providing significantly higher predictive accuracy than standard multi-channel visible-light (RGB) cameras. These findings demonstrate a promising, statistically validated framework that, upon further validation in operational climates, could support the optimization of heliostat aim-point strategies and real-time solar resource management in CSP facilities.  © 2026 The Authors.},
