	affiliations = {Qassim University, College of Engineering, Department of Electrical Engineering, Buraydah, 52571, Saudi Arabia; Qassim University, College of Computer, Department of Information Technology, Buraydah, 51452, Saudi Arabia},
	abstract = {Solar power is increasingly recognized as a pivotal alternative to mitigate the utilization of limited fossil fuels and their associated pollution. Photovoltaic (PV) power plants contribute substantially to renewable energy generation; however, they face challenges due to the intermittent and volatile nature of solar energy. Addressing these challenges necessitates an automatic and precise forecasting system to efficiently manage and ensure consistency in the electricity power system. Various deep learning-based techniques have been proposed for PV power forecasting, but there is still a pressing need to further reduce the error rates of these methods to effectively manage the grid. In this paper, we propose a novel hybrid deep learning-based network that combines dilated convolutional neural network (DCNN) and residual long short-term memory (RLSTM) techniques. The DCNN is used to extract spatial patterns from historical PV data under varying weather conditions. These patterns are then input into a bidirectional RLSTM network to capture temporal correlations within the data. The output of the DCNN-RLSTM network is then fed into fully connected layers for making predictions. Our proposed model’s performance is evaluated using various benchmarks and four different evaluation metrics: RMSE, MBE, MSE, and MAE. The results show that our model achieves lower error rates compared to previous models. This research contributes to the ongoing efforts in advancing renewable energy technologies for integration into energy systems and buildings. The proposed model uses innovative deep learning techniques to address key aspects of energy planning, management, and operation, ultimately supporting environmental sustainability, energy diversification, and reducing reliance on fossil fuels. © 2013 IEEE.},
