	abstract = {Building-integrated photovoltaics (BIPV) offer strong potential for net-zero energy buildings; however, envelope-level optimisation is computationally intensive due to conflicting objectives, high-dimensional variables, and costly simulations. Conventional surrogate models are typically problem-specific and lack transferability, requiring retraining for new design contexts. This study proposes a meta-learning–enhanced surrogate modelling framework for multi-objective optimisation of BIPV envelope design. A multi-output artificial neural network trained with a gradient-based meta-learning strategy learns transferable representations across diverse design tasks, enabling rapid adaptation to new building configurations with limited data. The surrogate model is integrated within a many-objective evolutionary optimisation framework and evaluated across multiple scenarios. Results demonstrate that the proposed approach maintains good predictive accuracy while significantly reducing computational time compared to simulation-based methods. It produces stable, well-distributed Pareto-optimal solutions that capture trade-offs among energy performance, life-cycle impact, daylight, glare, and thermal comfort. The findings highlight the potential of meta-learning surrogates for efficient and scalable building design optimisation. Future work will focus on improving data coverage and generalisation under more complex conditions. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier B.V.},
