	affiliations = {Faculty of Engineering and Pedagogy of Sliven, Technical University of Sofia, Sofia, 1767, Bulgaria},
	abstract = {Reliable forecasting of electrical load and photovoltaic generation is essential for operations, but the distribution level risk impact of forecast errors is seldom assessed directly. We present a pipeline that links 24 hour ahead forecasts for 1 to 24 hour horizons with quasi static time series simulations in OpenDSS on the IEEE123 test feeder. We use hourly ENTSO E data for Bulgaria 2015 to 2025 for national load and aggregated solar generation, enriched with ERA5 meteorology and solar position features. We benchmark persistence and seasonal lag baselines, a linear model, and deep models LSTM, TCN, and Transformer with 168 hour input. Forecasts are converted to feeder load scaling and PV injections distributed across automatically selected feeder buses. Simulations produce voltage and line loading metrics that feed risk indicators using rule based violations plus anomaly scores from PCA reconstruction error and Isolation Forest. The best mean MAE is 99.3 MW for load with Transformer and 63.0 MW for solar with TCN. After forecast-to-feeder mapping and OpenDSS simulation, voltage-limit violations remain limited, whereas line overloads emerge as the main stress mechanism across forecast-driven cases. We discuss the coupling between forecast behavior and feeder-level stress, together with the limitations of the benchmark feeder and the adopted scaling assumptions. © 2026 AECE.},
