	affiliations = {South Valley University, Faculty of Engineering, Mechanical Engineering Department, Qena, 83523, Egypt; Fahad Bin Sultan University, College of Engineering, Department of Mechanical Engineering, Tabuk, 47721, Saudi Arabia; South Valley University, Faculty of Engineering, Department of Electrical Engineering, Qena, 83523, Egypt; Assiut University, Department of Mechanical Engineering, Assiut, 71515, Egypt; King Khalid University, Faculty of Engineering, Department of Electrical Engineering, Abha, 61411, Saudi Arabia; Aswan University, Faculty of Engineering, Department of Electrical Engineering, Aswan, 81528, Egypt; Assiut University, Department of Electrical Engineering, Assiut, 71515, Egypt},
	abstract = {According to its various features, the solar photovoltaics (PV) system is realized as a significant promising energy source to cope with energy shortcomings and environmental impacts like contamination. Therefore, it is mandatory to estimate and predict the output power for prediction intervals to avoid any power outage or urgent disturbances in the utility grid. These are challenging tasks as the solar PV output power depends on the weather variables data such as temperature and solar radiation. In this article, the estimation and forecast of solar PV output power are investigated with an upscaling method using three different types of artificial neural networks (ANNs) in order to reduce the estimation errors in current types of ANNs. The multilayer feedforward neural network (MLFFNN), recurrent neural network (RNN), and nonlinear autoregressive exogenous (NARX) model neural network (NARXNN) are applied to estimate and forecast the total output power of four real solar PV substations in Egypt. Hence both the surface temperature and the solar radiation of each PV substation are applied as the inputs of each designed NN, whereas the total output power of the four PV substations is its output. For the training and effectiveness investigation procedures of each applied ANNs, the data of two months (60 days) are attained and collected from these four PV substations. Here, the data of the first 45 days are applied to train the three designed NNs, while the data from the remaining 15 days, which are not applied for the training, are used to check the effectiveness and the generalization capability of the trained NNs. Hence, the estimation process is considered a prior step for the forecast of the output power. Therefore, an upscaling method is utilized for assessing and forecasting a regional solar PV output power because of the limited number of monitored plants and applied data. The results provide evidence that the trained NNs are running very well and efficiently to estimate the power correctly. The performance of the MLFFNN is the best compared with the other NNs, whereas the NARXNN's performance is the lowest one. The MLFFNN achieves the lowest mean squared error (MSE) of 0.27533 and the lowest absolute approximation error of 0.2099 MWh. Finally, the assessment and comparison among the three trained NNs and other techniques in recently published articles are highlighted and presented which reveal the performance superiority of the three trained NNs compared to other ANNs.  © 2013 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {multilayer feedforward NN (MLFFNN); NN effectiveness and generalization ability; nonlinear autoregressive exogenous model neural network (NARXNN); Power prediction; recurrent neural network (RNN); solar PV power station},
	keywords = {Electric substations; Environmental impact; Forecasting; Mean square error; Meteorology; Multilayer neural networks; Multilayers; Recurrent neural networks; Solar power generation; Solar radiation; Autoregressive exogenous models; Generalization ability; Multilayer feedforward; Multilayer feedforward NN; NN effectiveness and generalization ability; Nonlinear autoregressive exogenous model neural network; Photovoltaic power stations; Power predictions; Power- generations; Prediction algorithms; Predictive models; Recurrent neural network; Solar photovoltaic power; Solar photovoltaic power station; Substation; Neural network models},
